"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][26]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M26-L1 – Graph Representation and Network Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe graphs using different\ndata representations and interpret local and global structural\nmeasures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Relationships rather than isolated rows<\/h2>\n<p>A library wants to analyse which research groups share equipment.\nNodes represent groups; an undirected edge means that two groups used at\nleast one piece of equipment jointly during the month under study. A\ngraph\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=(V,E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsists of a node set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an edge set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDirection, weight, timestamp and edge type are modelling decisions: a\nbank transfer is directed, whereas joint equipment use is symmetric.\nMultiple events can be stored as separate edges in a multigraph or as\nthe weight of an aggregated edge. These variants answer different\nquestions. Here, a missing edge means that no joint use was observed,\nnot necessarily that there was no contact.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnodes, a dense adjacency matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas dimensions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\times n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a simple undirected graph,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{ij}=A_{ji}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the diagonal is zero. When there are few edges, edge lists or sparse\nmatrices require considerably less storage; an adjacency list provides\ndirect access to outgoing edges. For very large graphs, partitioning and\nmemory access therefore often dominate computation time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A network with five groups<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001590_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Triangle A–B–C extends along path C–D–E, with node degrees 2, 2, 3, 2 and 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Triangle A–B–C extends along path C–D–E,\nwith node degrees 2, 2, 3, 2 and 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Nodes, edges and node degrees.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Groups A, B and C form a triangle; C connects to D, and D to E. The\nfive nodes have degrees\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2,2,3,2,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i d_i=2|E|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|E|=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density of a simple undirected graph is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|E|/[n(n-1)]=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe local clustering coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_i=2T_i/[d_i(d_i-1)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_i\\ge2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of edges between a node’s neighbours. For C it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor A and B it is 1 in each case. Here, we define the coefficient as\nzero when the degree is below two. The mean of the five local values is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7/15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the connections and each node’s degree.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros((<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i,j <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    A[i,j]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A[j,i]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>tri<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.diag(A<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>A<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>A)<span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.divide(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>tri,d<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(d<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),out<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),where<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> d.tolist()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(c.mean(),<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(d,c,c.mean())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Centrality and its limitations<\/h2>\n<p>High degree means many direct relationships, but not necessarily a\nbridging role. Betweenness counts what proportion of shortest paths\nbetween other nodes pass through the node being examined. If C is\nremoved, our graph splits into two components: C is an articulation\npoint. Removing A breaks the triangle but does not disconnect the graph.\nPageRank, by contrast, models visits by a random surfer who sometimes\njumps to a new node; weights and direction must be chosen appropriately.\nHigh centrality is neither proof of causation nor a judgement about the\nperson represented by a node. When comparing time windows, the node\npopulation and recording rules must remain consistent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Add the edge E–A. The density rises to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6/10=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nC is no longer an articulation point: after its removal, A–E–D connects\nthe remaining parts, and B is connected to A. This example shows how a\nsingle bridging edge can change fault tolerance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~wlh/grl_book/\">Hamilton (2020):\nGraph Representation Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview and table of contents,\nChapters 2–7<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M26-L2 – Graph Embeddings and Spectral Methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the graph Laplacian,\ninterpret spectral embeddings and distinguish them from random-walk\nmethods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning coordinates from relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A network of coupled sensors has no natural coordinate axis\ncomparable to a tabular feature. We want to represent nodes by short\nreal-valued vectors so that, for example, similar monitoring stations\ncan be analysed together. An embedding is a mapping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z:V\\to\\mathbb R^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSimilarity in embedding space must express the desired structural\nproperty: immediate proximity, membership of the same community or a\nsimilar role in different parts of the network. These objectives are not\nequivalent.<\/p>\n<p>For an undirected graph with symmetric non-negative weights,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{ii}=\\sum_j A_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=D-A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExpanding the expression for a real node vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the energy<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^\\top Lx=\\frac12\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}(x_i-x_j)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The sum is non-negative, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive semidefinite. Every constant vector belongs to its null\nspace. For a connected graph the null space is one-dimensional. The\neigenvector associated with the second-smallest eigenvalue minimises the\nenergy subject to unit norm and orthogonality to the constant vector. It\nis called the Fiedler vector. When degrees differ considerably, the\nnormalised variant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I-D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be more appropriate (assuming positive degrees here).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four sensors in a chain<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001707_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Path A–B–C–D has Fiedler coordinates −0.6533, −0.2706, 0.2706 and 0.6533.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Path A–B–C–D has Fiedler coordinates\n−0.6533, −0.2706, 0.2706 and 0.6533.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Spectral embedding of a path graph.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A–B–C–D is an unweighted path. The eigenvalues of the combinatorial\nLaplacian are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,2-\\sqrt2,2,2+\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUp to its arbitrary sign, the Fiedler vector is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.6533<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2706<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2706<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.6533<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-0.6533,-0.2706,0.2706,0.6533)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts signs separate A and B from C and D; the figure shows this\none-dimensional embedding on a number line. The sensors’ actual physical\npositions were not used.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.diag(np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.diag(np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>L<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.diag(A.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>A<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w,U<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.eigh(L)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>U[:,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> z[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>: z<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(w,[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(z<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>L<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>z,w[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(w.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>),z.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random walks and comparison<\/h2>\n<p>In a simple random walk, a neighbour is selected uniformly at each\nnode; the transition matrix is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=D^{-1}A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSequences of visited nodes can serve as training contexts for\nembeddings. node2vec changes transition weights according to the\npreceding node, allowing it to emphasise local or more distant contexts\ndifferently. Unlike the deterministic eigenvector approach, training\ninvolves stochastic decisions and an optimisation objective for context\nprediction. The resulting vectors are not automatically interpretable\none by one. With new nodes, many pure lookup embeddings require\nupdating; feature-based inductive models can instead process new nodes\nunder suitable assumptions. Link-prediction evaluation must not use\nheld-out edges when constructing the training representation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(1,1,-1,-1)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly the edge B–C contributes:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-(-1))^2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe squared norm is also 4, giving a Rayleigh quotient of 1, which is\ngreater than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus the optimal real-valued embedding need not be a step function.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~wlh/grl_book/\">Hamilton (2020):\nGraph Representation Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview and table of contents,\nChapters 2–7<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00653\">Grover and Leskovec\n(2016): node2vec: Scalable Feature Learning for Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M26-L3 – Graph Neural Networks and Message Passing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a message-passing\nlayer and assess receptive range, permutation behaviour and model\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Neighbourhoods as learnable inputs<\/h2>\n<p>In a logistics network, each warehouse has a utilisation value and\nother features. Predicting a bottleneck should also take neighbouring\nsites into account. A graph neural network (GNN) computes\nrepresentations on the network using shared parameters. A common scheme\nconsists of messages\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_{j\\to i}=\\phi(h_i,h_j,e_{ji})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan aggregation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">AGG<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_i=\\operatorname{AGG}_{j\\in N(i)}m_{j\\to i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an update\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&#39;_i=\\psi(h_i,a_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSummation, averaging or suitably defined attention processes neighbours\nindependently of their order. A node index is generally not a meaningful\nfeature in its own right.<\/p>\n<p>In node classification, each node receives an output. In graph\nclassification, invariant pooling combines node representations into one\ngraph representation. Link prediction instead uses a scoring function on\npairs or relation triples. These tasks require different target\nvariables and test splits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating a layer by hand<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001645_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Inputs 2, 8 and 2 become averages 5, 4 and 5, then 4.5, 4.667 and 4.5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Inputs 2, 8 and 2 become averages 5, 4\nand 5, then 4.5, 4.667 and 4.5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two message-passing layers.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The three warehouses A–B–C form a chain. The initial features are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(2,8,2)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA deliberately simple layer averages each node’s own value with all its\ndirect neighbours, without any trainable transformation. For the end\nnodes, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2+8)/2=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor the middle node,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2+8+2)/3=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter another layer, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4.5,14/3,4.5)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows information flow and the outputs of both steps. More\ngenerally, a GCN with self-loops uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde A=A+I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde D_{ii}=\\sum_j\\tilde A_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H&#39;=\\sigma(\\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}HW).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Symmetric normalisation differs from our row-wise mean.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransforms the features, and non-linear activations enable more complex\nfunctions. For the row-mean variant we additionally check permutation\nequivariance: renumbering the nodes changes only the order of the\noutputs.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>H<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.eye(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>P<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>B.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,keepdims<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h1<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>P<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>H<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> h2<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>P<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>h1<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>idx<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(h1,[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(h2,[<span class=\"fl\">4.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(P[np.ix_(idx,idx)]<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>H[idx],h1[idx])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h1,h2)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training and problematic depth<\/h2>\n<p>For supervised tasks, a loss function is optimised using the\navailable training labels. The network may use unlabelled neighbours as\ninput if the evaluation definition permits it. Transductive evaluation\non a known graph is not the same as inductive prediction for sites added\nin the future. In particular, a temporal split must exclude future edges\nand features. After\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlocal layers, the receptive field extends no more than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nedges. Repeated averaging can reduce differences (oversmoothing); many\ndistant signals may also be compressed into vectors that are too small\n(oversquashing). Residual connections, sampling and a suitable\narchitecture may help, depending on the task, but do not replace\ncomparison with a non-graph-based model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What influence does C have on A after exactly one of the defined\naveraging layers? None, because the nodes are not adjacent. After two\nlayers, the coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(P^2)_{AC}=P_{AB}P_{BC}=1/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIncreasing C’s new measurement by six units therefore raises A’s output\nafter two layers by exactly one unit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~wlh/grl_book/\">Hamilton (2020):\nGraph Representation Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview and table of contents,\nChapters 2–7<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://tkipf.github.io/graph-convolutional-networks/\">Kipf\n(2016): Graph Convolutional Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · tutorial page opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M26-L4 – Collaborative Filtering and Matrix Factorisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish explicit from\nimplicit interactions and follow a regularised factorisation update.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning from shared behaviour<\/h2>\n<p>An educational portal offers short exercises in statistics and\nprogramming. Ratings are available for some users, but only clicks for\nothers. Content-based recommendations use features of the exercises;\ncollaborative methods use common patterns of interaction. Neighbourhood\nmethods may identify similar users or similar exercises. However, high\nestimated similarity from only a few shared observations is unreliable.\nMoreover, a click is not a direct measure of satisfaction. Exercises\nthat were never opened may never have been shown; missing ratings must\nnot simply be treated as zeros.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Latent factors and one training step<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001639_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Bars compare rating predictions of 2.5 and 3.435 with a target rating of 4.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bars compare rating predictions of 2.5\nand 3.435 with a target rating of 4.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: One step of matrix factorisation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For user\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand exercise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe use the prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat r_{ui}=\\mu+b_u+b_i+p_u^\\top q_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a global level,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_u,b_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare deviations and the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-dimensional\nvectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_u,q_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent latent factors. A simple squared-error objective over the\nobserved pairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\frac12\\sum_{(u,i)\\in\\Omega}(r_{ui}-\\hat r_{ui})^2+\\frac\\lambda2\\left(\\sum_u\\|p_u\\|^2+\\sum_i\\|q_i\\|^2\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Consider the single case without biases:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(1,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand observed rating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat r=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=r-\\hat r=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand learning rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&#39;=p+\\eta(e q-\\lambda p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;=q+\\eta(e p-\\lambda q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth updates use the old values. We therefore obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.29<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.645<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&#39;=(1.29,0.645)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.065<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;=(2.13,1.065)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe new predicted rating is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.434625<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.434625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the observed value and both predictions. The step\nreduces the error for this one pair; it does not imply improved\ngeneralisation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>q<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(e<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> qq<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(e<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(pp,[<span class=\"fl\">1.29<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.645<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(qq,[<span class=\"fl\">2.13<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.065<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(pp<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>qq,<span class=\"fl\">3.434625<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>q,pp<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>qq)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training and the cold-start problem<\/h2>\n<p>Stochastic gradient descent processes individual observations or\nmini-batches. Alternating least squares optimises either user factors or\nitem factors while keeping the other side fixed. For implicit\ninteractions, positive events may receive higher weights, or a model may\nlearn preferences between item pairs. The choice of negative examples\naffects the result; randomly selected unobserved pairs are not\nguaranteed negative labels. New exercises have no interaction data yet\n(item cold start). Content features, editorial recommendations or\ncontrolled exploration can mitigate this. RMSE is useful for rating\nscales, whereas ranking metrics are often more direct for top-k\nrecommendations. The latent axes can change under a common orthogonal\ntransformation without changing the dot products, so verbal labels\nshould not be attached to individual factors uncritically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A second exercise has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_2=(0.5,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBefore the update,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^\\top q_2=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafterwards,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi><\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.935<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&#39;^\\top q_2=1.935<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEven exercises not involved in the update may receive new scores because\nthe user’s representation has changed. Selecting one training example\ntherefore affects many later recommendations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/MC.2009.263\">Koren, Bell and\nVolinsky (2009): Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender\nSystems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · publisher record<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~wlh/grl_book/\">Hamilton (2020):\nGraph Representation Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview and table of contents,\nChapters 2–7<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M26-L5 – Ranking, Recommendations and Feedback Loops<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Measure ranking quality and\ndistinguish feedback, exposure bias and evaluation design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selection and ordering<\/h2>\n<p>An educational portal contains a thousand exercises. A candidate\ngenerator first selects a smaller set of suitable exercises, a ranker\norders them, and the interface displays a subset. Candidate generation,\nranking and presentation jointly determine which interactions can be\nobserved. Even the best rating predictor is insufficient if relevant\nexercises were already excluded in the first stage. Offline metrics\nmeasure quality relative to known test relevance labels. For binary\nrelevance, Precision@k is the number of relevant hits among the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitems divided by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nRecall@k divides by the total number of test items known to be relevant.\nBoth require meaningfully defined candidates and test labels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graded relevance and nDCG<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001675_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Observed ranking 1, 3, 0, 2 versus ideal 3, 2, 1, 0, with nDCG@3 of 0.5767.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Observed ranking 1, 3, 0, 2 versus ideal\n3, 2, 1, 0, with nDCG@3 of 0.5767.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Relevance of two ranked lists.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A user is offered four exercises. Their graded test relevances in\ndisplayed order are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,3,0,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing a gain function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(r)=2^r-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a logarithmic rank discount, we define<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>@<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/munderover><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>@<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>@<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>@<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">DCG@k=\\sum_{j=1}^k\\frac{2^{r_j}-1}{\\log_2(j+1)},\\qquad nDCG@k=\\frac{DCG@k}{IDCG@k}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">IDCG<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the value for the ideal ordering of the same candidates. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.4165<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">DCG=1+7/\\log_2 3\\approx5.4165<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.3928<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">IDCG=7+3/\\log_2 3+1/2\\approx9.3928<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe nDCG is approximately 0.5767. The figure compares the two rankings.\nIf there are no relevant items, the denominator is zero; the treatment\nof such cases must be documented for each benchmark.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> dcg(r,k):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>log2(j<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j,v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(r[:k]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>dcg(r,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>dcg(<span class=\"bu\">sorted<\/span>(r,reverse<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.576<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.577<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(a,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(b,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feedback in data collection<\/h2>\n<p>The portal frequently shows popular exercises, receives more\ninteractions with them as a result, and subsequently trains on those\ninteractions. Popularity may be reinforced while new exercises remain\nunseen. A high score on later data produced by the same display policy\ndoes not necessarily demonstrate improved learning outcomes. Users can\ninteract only with content that was displayed. Where possible, record\ncandidates, impressions, time, position and the policy used, subject to\ndata-minimisation requirements. With randomised exploration and known\npositive selection probabilities, inverse probability weights can\nevaluate a differently distributed target policy; very small\nprobabilities, however, lead to high variance. An A/B test needs a\nwell-defined learning objective, duration and guardrail metrics.\nDiversity, dropout rates, longer-term learning outcomes and fair\nexposure can matter alongside clicks. A temporal split prevents future\ninformation leakage but does not remove selection bias.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Of 100 random impressions for a new type of exercise, 20 lead to\nclicks; for an older type, 90 of 900 impressions lead to clicks. The\nolder type receives more clicks, but the observed click-through rates\nare 10% versus 20%. Even this is not reliable evidence of a causal\ndifference without comparable audiences and positions. Compare under\nequal randomised exposure and with a separate measure of actual learning\nprogress.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11214\">Chaney, Stewart and\nEngelhardt (2018): How Algorithmic Confounding in Recommendation Systems\nIncreases Homogeneity and Decreases Utility<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/MC.2009.263\">Koren, Bell and\nVolinsky (2009): Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender\nSystems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · publisher record<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M26-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M26-L6 – Knowledge Graphs and Neuro-Symbolic Approaches<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Graphs, Recommender Systems and Structured Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M17, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish relation triples,\nsymbolic inferences and neural scores, and combine them\nappropriately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Structured statements and inference<\/h2>\n<p>A workshop database needs to establish which component belongs to\nwhich machine. A knowledge graph stores typed relations, such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TeilVon<\/mtext><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Motor7,\\text{TeilVon},Pumpe2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TeilVon<\/mtext><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Pumpe2,\\text{TeilVon},Anlage4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnlike a simple untyped graph, it distinguishes entities and different\npredicates. A schema can define permitted entity types, relations and\nconstraints. If the relation TeilVon (‘part of’) is explicitly defined\nas transitive in the model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TeilVon<\/mtext><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Motor7,\\text{TeilVon},Anlage4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows logically. Not every relation is transitive: two maintenance\norders, for example, do not automatically imply a third. Under an\nopen-world assumption, a statement that is not stored is not\nautomatically false; negating it requires additional information.\nProvenance and time are essential when facts can change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Vector scores for possible relations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001737_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Head h=(1,1) and relation r=(2,0) predict candidate A=(3,1) with zero error; B=(3,3) has error 2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Head h=(1,1) and relation r=(2,0) predict\ncandidate A=(3,1) with zero error; B=(3,3) has error 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Translation in embedding space.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An embedding model such as TransE scores triples by comparing a head\nvector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrelation vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand tail vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne possible error score is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(h,r,t)=\\|h+r-t\\|_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere smaller values mean greater plausibility according to the model.\nSet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=(2,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand consider candidates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_A=(3,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_B=(3,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe errors are 0 and 2. The figure illustrates the translation and the\nremaining distance. This is a teaching configuration, not a trained\nrepresentation. Among other limitations, TransE struggles with certain\none-to-many or symmetric relations; other embedding families model\nrelations more flexibly. Training needs, for example, positive triples\nand deliberately corrupted triples; unobserved triples may still be\ntrue.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> T<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.norm(h<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>T,axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(s,[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>facts<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Motor7&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Pumpe2&#39;<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Pumpe2&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Anlage4&#39;<\/span>)}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>closure<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>facts.copy()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    extra<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{(a,d) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> closure <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> c,d <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> closure <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>c}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> extra<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>closure: <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    closure<span class=\"op\">|=<\/span>extra<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&#39;Motor7&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Anlage4&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> closure<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(s,<span class=\"bu\">sorted<\/span>(closure))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Combining two types of statements<\/h2>\n<p>A neuro-symbolic system may first generate neural candidates and then\ncheck them against type or safety rules. Alternatively, rule violations\ncan be included as soft penalties in a learning objective. A hard filter\nis appropriate for genuinely binding requirements, but it is only as\nreliable as its rule set and input data. Soft penalties do not guarantee\nlogical consistency. A symbolically derived statement has a traceable\nchain of evidence; a neural score is a ranking value, not a logical\ntruth and, without separate calibration, not a probability. Evaluation\nmust treat explicit facts, logical consequences and model suggestions\nseparately. In link prediction, filtered ranking avoids penalising\nalternative correct triples already known to be true. For temporal\ngraphs, test times must again be specified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A neural model additionally proposes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TeilVon<\/mtext><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Anlage4,\\text{TeilVon},Motor7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf TeilVon is defined as a strict part-of relation that is irreflexive\nand transitive, the proposal contradicts irreflexivity when combined\nwith the inferred relation in the opposite direction: Motor7 would\nbecome part of itself. The system should flag or reject the proposal,\nnot store it as an established fact because its score is favourable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~wlh/grl_book/\">Hamilton (2020):\nGraph Representation Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview and table of contents,\nChapters 2–7<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2013/hash/1cecc7a77928ca8133fa24680a88d2f9-Abstract.html\">Bordes\net al. (2013): Translating Embeddings for Modeling Multi-relational\nData<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07200\">DeLong, Fernández Mir and\nFleuriot (2023): Neurosymbolic AI for Reasoning over Knowledge Graphs: A\nSurvey<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
